# In-application backup storage and downloads

This document describes the local-file and WebDAV transfer implementation. It
does not certify a deployment, replace a database/volume recovery plan, or extend
these limits to every storage provider, export, import or decryption operation.
The `pg-backup` database-dump service has a separate lifecycle and policy.

## Transfer storage and capacity

Catalogue downloads through the local-file and WebDAV paths use an independently
owned disk snapshot. A snapshot is copied, read back and checked before delivery.
Transfer buffers are at most 64 KiB per read; the archive limit is 200 MiB. A saved
whole-file identity is verified when available. Legacy archives without that
identity remain explicitly unverified; copying or hashing one does not invent
historical authenticity or prove that it is safe to restore.

The API uses these settings:

| Setting | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `BACKUP_DOWNLOAD_SPOOL` | `/data/backups/.transfers` | Shared local disk directory for transfer policy, admission locks and temporary snapshots. |
| `BACKUP_DOWNLOAD_SLOTS` | `4` | Concurrent adopted downloads sharing this directory; valid range 1–16. |
| `BACKUP_DOWNLOAD_FREE_BYTES` | `1073741824` | Free-space floor used in the conservative admission calculation. |
| `BACKUP_DOWNLOAD_SECONDS` | `900` | Deadline from response adoption through preparation and outer application delivery; valid range 1–86400 seconds. |

The spool requires Linux mount metadata and an actual ext4, xfs, btrfs or zfs
filesystem. Memory-backed `/tmp`, overlay filesystems, NFS and other filesystem
types are refused for the spool. Use a suitable disk-backed volume. A configured
archive location may be separate from the spool; an NFS archive location does
not make NFS a supported transfer spool or establish NFS durability.

All API processes sharing a spool must use the same slot count, size limit and
free-space policy. On admission, the current available space must cover the
configured floor **plus slot count × maximum archive size**. With the defaults,
that is 1 GiB plus 800 MiB. This is a check, not an exclusive filesystem space
reservation: unrelated writers can still exhaust the disk.

Capacity is shared through file locks, without a queue waiting for a transfer
slot. A busy metadata lock can wait for up to one second. Capacity refusal is
HTTP 503 with `Retry-After: 5`. The adopted request keeps its admission ownership
through outer application delivery and mandatory cleanup, including small
archives whose disk snapshot has already reached EOF.

Do not delete policy or slot files while any process may still use the spool;
replacing a locked inode can defeat shared admission. Coordinate configuration
changes across all API processes. Preserve the previous directory and policy
until all owners have stopped and recovery has been assessed; a new, correctly
owned spool may be configured consistently for the replacement processes.
Different spool paths create separate pools, not a host-wide capacity limit.

## Existing archive paths and permissions

Local archive operations walk relative path components with pinned directory
descriptors and do not follow symlinks. Absolute paths, empty or dot components,
parent traversal, special files and multiply linked archive files are refused.
Archive directories and files must have supported ownership: root or the
application's effective user. World-writable objects are refused; group-writable
objects require the application's primary group. The implementation has a
limited root-owned sticky-ancestor exception, not a general writable-root policy.
Existing modes and ownership are inspected, never silently repaired.

New archive files and pool metadata are mode 0600. Pool files specifically must
belong to the effective application user and have no group/other permissions.
Processes sharing that user or trusted primary group, and privileged operators,
remain trusted writers; these checks do not isolate hostile same-UID processes.
The shipped API/worker identity is UID/GID 1001. Verify actual volume ownership
and mounts before an upgrade; native test fixtures do not qualify your volumes.

Before changing ownership, permissions or archive layout, stop the relevant
writers, preserve a separate copy, inspect the exact refused path and verify a
restore on an isolated target. Do not use recursive permission changes or alias
replacement as an automatic repair. An old path being rejected is not proof that
the archive is corrupt or disposable.

## Failures, cancellation and integrity

Local saves use a unique owned temporary file, file synchronization, replacement
and directory synchronization. If a complete destination exists but final
publication cannot be acknowledged, the backup remains failed with its locator
retained. It must not be reported as completed, verified or rolled back merely
because an error occurred. Preserve the object for operator recovery; a failed
catalogue row is not permission to delete a potentially useful backup.

Local/WebDAV download authority is checked again after preparation, against the
current account, initiating credential, catalogue and storage state. This does
not revoke bytes already delivered. Full-vault downloads retain their separate
role/credential rules; these checks are not purpose-bound fresh authentication.

WebDAV operations own and close their clients, retain pinned destination and
TLS hostname checks, refuse redirects, and bound response data. Their operation
deadline is 60 seconds, separate from the adopted download deadline above.
Neither setting is a guarantee that a blocked kernel operation can be forcibly
stopped safely.

A preparation timeout before response headers returns HTTP 504 with the normal
error and request headers. After headers have started, the application terminates
with an error rather than sending a second status or a fabricated success body.
Cancellation drains owned filesystem work and cleanup before releasing its
resources. If cleanup cannot be confirmed, investigate the retained diagnostic
and failed operation; do not infer durable recovery from a disconnected client.

## Limits still requiring deployment acceptance

These paths do not bound arbitrary storage SDK buffers, every concurrent API
request, proxy/client buffering, exports, decryptors or uploads. Shared Docker
volumes, NFS behavior, external disconnects, full configured deadlines, process
or host loss, quotas, other providers, and measured recovery objectives require
their own acceptance. No complete resource budget, high-availability guarantee,
independent security audit or enterprise production clearance is implied.
